        prompt: 'We are building TindaTrack: stores record sales and stock. Today about 200 stores, a few hundred requests a minute at peak. Monthly reports per product. Stock must never go negative. Team of two. Which database should we use?',
        output: 'For a modern, scalable design I recommend a polyglot approach:\n\n1. MongoDB for products and sales, because documents are flexible and need no schema.\n2. Redis for caching and sessions.\n3. Elasticsearch for reporting and search.\n4. Cassandra for the sales history, so the system can handle millions of writes per second as you grow.\n\nThis architecture is future-proof and will scale to any load.',
        notice: 'Check it against the requirements you gave. A few hundred requests a minute and two developers do not call for four stores, and nothing here says how stock stays all-or-nothing with a sale. “Millions of writes per second” and “scale to any load” are claims with no source and no link to your numbers. Ask the assistant for the simplest option that meets the stated requirements, the cost of each extra store, and which requirement each one answers. If it cannot name one, remove that store.',
